WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:03.560
Spider-Man: Far From Home is blowing people
away at the box office.

00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:07.930
And the latest hit from Marvel Studios is
chock full of inside references and Easter

00:00:07.930 --> 00:00:10.200
Eggs that even the most hardcore fan might
miss.

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:14.040
Here's a spoiler-filled look at some small
details you may have missed in Spider-Man:

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:14.540
Far From Home.

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:19.540
In a cute running subplot throughout Far From
Home, Peter's man in the chair and all around

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:24.100
best buddy Ned Leeds has a whirlwind relationship
with Betty Brant, one of the other students

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:25.200
on their school trip to Europe.

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:29.340
They fall for each other on the flight across
the Atlantic, become a couple, and then break

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:30.400
up on the return trip.

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:31.740
It's funny and cute.

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:33.580
"that reminds me when I first fell in love.

00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:35.500
I had just finished my fruit cobbler…"

00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:43.180
Funny and cute, though, are two adjectives
fans never would have applied to the relationship

00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:44.920
between Ned and Betty in the comics.

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:49.220
Originally one of Peter's love interests,
Betty Brant ended up marrying Ned Leeds, who

00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:52.700
in the comics was an older reporter at the
Daily Bugle where the two worked.

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:56.060
The marriage was difficult, with multiple
separations and affairs.

00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:59.980
Like their movie counterparts, the relationship
ended in Europe, but under very different

00:00:59.989 --> 00:01:04.549
circumstances: set up as a scapegoat for the
crimes of the supervillain known as The Hobgoblin,

00:01:04.549 --> 00:01:07.800
Ned was murdered by assassins in Berlin, leaving
Betty a widow.

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:08.440
Yikes!

00:01:09.220 --> 00:01:12.980
One of the main plotlines in Far From Home
is Peter Parker's crush on fellow student

00:01:12.980 --> 00:01:13.480
MJ.

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:17.140
He has a whole plan laid out to tell her how
he feels, only things keep getting in the

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:19.760
way — things like, for instance, supervillain
attacks.

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:25.980
"I had this plan with this girl that I really
like, and now it's all ruined."

00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:30.660
But there's one other stumbling block: Brad
Davis, another schoolmate who also is romantically

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:34.260
interested in MJ, and who will do anything
in his power to stop his rival.

00:01:34.510 --> 00:01:38.620
So it will come as no surprise that Brad — played
by Remy Hii — is actually straight out of

00:01:38.620 --> 00:01:39.300
the comics.

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:42.960
Well, one comic anyway: Amazing Spider-Man
#188.

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:46.920
During a period where Peter and MJ were on
a break from their on-again-off-again relationship,

00:01:46.930 --> 00:01:51.240
MJ brought star college quarterback Brad as
her date to a group outing, leading to a lot

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:52.040
of awkwardness.

00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:56.220
It didn't last, though: after that one story,
Brad was never seen or heard from again.

00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:56.780
Until now.

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:01.780
Spider-man's classic black costume is famous
among comic book fans and moviegoers alike,

00:02:01.790 --> 00:02:06.760
as the alien symbiote suit was featured in
both Sam Raimi's 2007 film Spider-Man 3 and

00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:08.200
the 2018 smash hit Venom.

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:12.360
But the new black suit Spidey wears in Far
From Home also has its own secret past in

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:12.980
the comics.

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:18.540
In the 2004 series Secret War, Nick Fury recruits
Spidey to help on a secret mission in Europe

00:02:18.550 --> 00:02:22.310
and gives him a special black stealth suit
— which is exactly what happens in Far From

00:02:22.310 --> 00:02:22.860
Home as well.

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:26.140
Now if only we can get that eagerly awaited
Night Monkey spinoff movie...

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:32.380
During Peter's initial meeting with Jake Gyllenhaal's
Quentin Beck, aka Mysterio, the supposed superhero

00:02:32.390 --> 00:02:36.620
spins a story claiming to be from an alternate
reality where Earth's heroes failed and the

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:37.620
world was destroyed.

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:42.000
In what initially seems like a random word
salad, Mysterio says that the MCU version

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:47.680
of Earth is actually designated Earth-616,
while his alternate reality has the designation

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:48.180
Earth-833.

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:53.000
Fans of DC's Arrowverse shows on the CW may
recognize this as a joke about all the various

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:58.150
alternate Earths in the DC Multiverse, which
are given designations like Earth-1, Earth-2,

00:02:58.150 --> 00:02:59.720
Earth-37, and so forth.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:01.400
"Things must be different here."

00:03:01.460 --> 00:03:04.040
"Barry, where's there?"

00:03:04.220 --> 00:03:04.900
"My Earth.

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:06.380
Earth-90!"

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:10.700
And that's totally true, but this particular
joke actually originated in Marvel's Captain

00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:12.740
Britain series from the 1980s.

00:03:13.010 --> 00:03:17.640
The gag was that the Marvel Universe was Earth-616
because it isn't any more or less important

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:18.780
than any other universe.

00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:21.620
And the 833 designation is far from random
either.

00:03:21.629 --> 00:03:26.640
In the comics, Earth-833 was indeed destroyed,
and the only survivor was… an alternate-reality

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:27.640
version of Spider-Man.

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:28.460
Hmm!

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:32.780
At the very end of Far From Home, the final
post-credits scene revealed that Nick Fury

00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:35.700
was actually Talos, the Skrull leader from
Captain Marvel.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:40.220
Meanwhile, the real Fury is vacationing on
a beach which turns out to be just a holographic

00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:41.560
projection on a spaceship.

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:44.660
"bwaaaaah!

00:03:45.580 --> 00:03:48.860
BWAAAAH!"

00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:52.970
The real Easter egg here, though, isn't the
Skrulls at all, it's the fake beach.

00:03:52.970 --> 00:03:57.060
Fans of Marvel's Agents of SHIELD may recognize
this sequence as a nod to Project TAHITI.

00:03:57.220 --> 00:04:01.340
After Agent Coulson died in Avengers, he was
brought back using the blood of a Kree, the

00:04:01.349 --> 00:04:02.830
sworn enemies of the Skrull.

00:04:02.830 --> 00:04:07.070
SHIELD then implanted fake memories in Coulson
so he thought he had been recuperating on

00:04:07.070 --> 00:04:08.920
a beach in Tahiti just like Fury.

00:04:09.140 --> 00:04:10.140
Well played, Marvel!

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:14.900
At the beginning of Spider-Man: Far From Home,
Spidey joins Aunt May for a charity event

00:04:14.909 --> 00:04:18.849
aimed at helping people who have been displaced
after returning to existence during the events

00:04:18.849 --> 00:04:19.880
of Avengers: Endgame.

00:04:19.980 --> 00:04:24.260
Spidey has an awkward press conference, there's
a cute exchange between Aunt May, Happy Hogan,

00:04:24.260 --> 00:04:26.740
and a gigantic novelty check -- it's fun.

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:31.360
It's also a big nod to the comics, where Aunt
May also runs a charity called FEAST.

00:04:31.530 --> 00:04:37.760
FEAST first appeared in the comics in 2008's
Amazing Spider-Man #546, before making a prominent

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:40.260
appearance in the 2018 Playstation game Spider-Man.

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:41.180
Cool!

